-- title:
--   Conjugation preserves archimedean bi-finite factorizable test functions
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a number field, and work on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adele ring, `AdelicGL2 (𝓞 F) F`. Let $g$ lie in `finiteAdelicGL2Subgroup F`, the kernel of the archimedean projection [`NumberField.AdelicLevel.glArch (𝓞 F) F`](def/NumberField_AdelicLevel.html#L191), so $g$ has trivial archimedean component; let $k$ lie in the supremum, over the infinite places $w$ of $F$, of the ranges of the homomorphisms `rowIsometryInclAt₀ F w`, i.e. in the subgroup generated by the archimedean row-isometry subgroups $\mathrm{rowIsometrySubgroup₀}$ at the infinite places. Let $f : \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F) \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfy `IsFactorizableTestFn F f`: there are $f_\infty$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the infinite adeles and $f_{\mathrm f}$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the finite adeles with $f(y) = f_\infty(\mathrm{glArch}\,y)\cdot f_{\mathrm f}(\mathrm{glFin}\,y)$, where $f_\infty$ is given by a $C^\infty$ function of the matrix entries in mixed space composed with `archEntries F` and has compact support, and $f_{\mathrm f}$ is locally constant with compact support. Assume further `IsArchBiFinite F tys f` for some archimedean type family `tys` (a cardinality function on infinite places together with representations of the row-isometry subgroups of the completions), that is, $x \mapsto f(x^{-1})$ lies in `archCutSubmodule F tys` and $f$ lies in `archDualCutSubmodule F tys`, the indicated intersections over infinite places of sums of the type submodules attached to the chosen representations. Then the conjugate $y \mapsto f((gk)\,y\,(gk)^{-1})$ is again a factorizable test function, and is archimedean bi-finite for some type family `tys'` (not asserted to be `tys`).
--
--   This records the stability, under conjugation by an element with trivial archimedean part times a product of archimedean row-isometry elements, of the class of factorizable test functions that are bi-finite for the archimedean type conditions; conjugation acts through the finite factor by $g$ and through the archimedean factor by $k$, and the type family is allowed to change. It is used in the construction of cuspidal constituents, where a cyclic subrepresentation generated by such a test function is shown to lie in the cusp space ([`AutomorphicForm.CuspidalConstituent.isCuspSubrep_span_cyclic_and_mem_and_le`](thm.html#AutomorphicForm.CuspidalConstituent.isCuspSubrep_span_cyclic_and_mem_and_le)).
